<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/foundational/memory">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson sits early in Psychology, in the unit on the cognitive approach. You need
    nothing from earlier lessons — only a memory, which you have been running your whole
    life without an owner's manual. This page is the manual.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the main assumptions of the cognitive approach and explain how psychologists study processes they cannot see;</li>
    <li>describe the multi-store model — sensory store, working memory, long-term memory — and trace a memory through it;</li>
    <li>name the three jobs of any memory system (encoding, storage, retrieval) and the three ways information comes back out (recall, recognition, relearning);</li>
    <li>outline the working-memory model and use the Andrade doodling study to show how its parts can be tested;</li>
    <li>read the forgetting curve, and list the techniques that genuinely fight it — spacing, retrieval practice, sleep — plus the ones that only feel like they work;</li>
    <li>explain why memory is reconstructive, and what the Loftus-and-Palmer and Pozzulo line-up studies prove about eyewitnesses;</li>
    <li>describe the "Reading the Mind in the Eyes" test as an example of the cognitive method applied to social thinking.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a claim about the mind appears, we ask what <em>task</em> and what
    <em>measure</em> produced the evidence. Memory research is a masterclass in making
    invisible processes visible.
  </p>

  <h2>The birthday cake and the chain of stores</h2>
  <p>
    Try to remember your last birthday. Whatever surfaces — a cake, a face, a fragment of
    song — did not arrive in your mind like a video playing back. Somewhere in your brain, a
    physical trace was built when the day happened, stored for months, and reassembled just
    now into something that feels like a memory. Understanding how that building, storing and
    rebuilding works is the whole business of memory research, and it matters far beyond the
    laboratory. It explains why cramming fails, why confident eyewitnesses can still be
    wrong, why doodling during a boring phone call can be smart, and why a person who cannot
    form new memories can still learn a new skill.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founding insight of the <strong>cognitive approach</strong> — the school of
    psychology this lesson belongs to — is that between the world coming in and behaviour
    coming out, something processes information. Cognitive psychologists treat the mind as
    an information-processing system: like a computer, it takes input (light, sound, touch),
    <strong>encodes</strong> it (translates it into a form the system can keep),
    <strong>stores</strong> it, and later <strong>retrieves</strong> it. Because the
    processing is internal, it cannot be watched directly. Instead, researchers
    <em>infer</em> it: they measure behaviour carefully — how much is recalled, how fast a
    decision is made, which errors people make — and design experiments that let one
    explanation win over another. The approach also assumes the mind uses
    <strong>schemas</strong>: organised packages of prior knowledge that shape what we
    notice and how we interpret it. Those assumptions drive everything that follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Memory itself is not one thing but a chain of <strong>three systems in series</strong>.
    The <strong>sensory store</strong> holds raw impressions for under a second — a flash,
    an echo. What you pay attention to moves into <strong>working memory</strong>, the
    conscious workspace that keeps a handful of items alive for 15–30 seconds unless you
    keep rehearsing them. What gets elaborated and connected can then be filed into
    <strong>long-term memory</strong>, whose capacity is, as far as anyone has been able to
    measure, effectively unlimited — measured not in megabytes but in years. This chain is
    the <strong>multi-store model</strong> of Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968), and it remains
    the map on which almost every memory finding gets pinned.
  </p>
  <p>
    Encoding, the first job, comes in two styles. <strong>Automatic processing</strong>
    files away time, space and frequency with no effort at all — you know roughly what you
    ate for breakfast and that you have seen this person twice this week without ever
    trying. <strong>Effortful processing</strong> is everything you study: it demands
    attention and work, which is exactly why it can be helped or sabotaged by technique. A
    memory is not a gift you have or lack; it is the output of a system whose inputs you can
    control.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "some people simply have a good memory and some people
  a bad one, like a fixed camera quality." It feels true because differences between people
  are so visible. But memory is a set of processes, not a single talent — capacity is
  largely fixed and unremarkable in everyone, while encoding strategy, retrieval practice
  and sleep vary enormously and dominate real-world performance. The students who "have
  good memories" are almost always running better techniques, and techniques can be
  taught.</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ebbinghaus's forgetting curve — raise the strength slider (more review, better sleep) and watch decay flatten" widget="plotter" config={forgetCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> every curve on the panel is the same shape — a plunge
    in the first hours, then a long flattening tail — and the only thing the slider changes
    is the speed. With memory strength s set to 1 day, retention has collapsed before
    breakfast on day two; at 30 days, over half survives a month. Nothing on this panel can
    make the curve <em>not</em> fall: forgetting is the default. Every study technique that
    works is secretly fighting for a bigger <em>s</em>, and we will meet them in the
    forgetting section below.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the mind encodes, stores and retrieves; the multi-store model
    splits storage into a one-second buffer, a small conscious desk, and a vast archive. Now
    we zoom into the desk, because it is where attention — the system's true bottleneck —
    does its work.
  </p>

  <h2>Working memory: a desk, not a box</h2>
  <p>
    The multi-store model treated short-term memory as a passive bucket with about seven
    slots. In 1956, George Miller noticed a strange regularity in everyday cognition: people
    can hold roughly <strong>7 ± 2 items</strong> in mind at once — seven digits, seven
    words, seven objects. But later work showed the bucket leaks and misbehaves in ways a
    single box cannot explain. Someone can simultaneously repeat a phone number <em>and</em>
    picture a route home, yet the same person cannot listen to two people talking at once.
    If short-term memory were one store, all tasks would compete for it equally. They don't.
  </p>
  <p>
    Baddeley and Hitch (1974) therefore split the workspace into a <strong>working
    memory</strong> with parts: a <em>phonological loop</em> that rehearses sound and speech
    (the inner voice), a <em>visuospatial sketchpad</em> that manipulates images and spatial
    layouts (the inner eye), and a <em>central executive</em> — the attention manager that
    decides what the two loops work on. A fourth component, the <em>episodic buffer</em>,
    was added in 2000 to bind the streams together into single episodes that can be handed
    to long-term memory. The model explains the everyday juggling act: you can hum a tune
    while reading a map (different stores), but you cannot read and hold a conversation
    (both compete for one).
  </p>
  <p>
    Jackie Andrade's <strong>doodling study</strong> (2009) turned this architecture into a
    practical surprise. Participants listened to a deliberately dull mock telephone message
    listing who would attend a party; one group was told to shade printed shapes (doodle)
    while listening, the other just listened. On a surprise memory test, doodlers recalled
    about 29% more — roughly 7.5 of the names and places versus 5.8 for the listeners — and
    were better at spotting the names of people who would <em>not</em> attend. The mechanism
    is exactly what working-memory theory predicts: a boring listening task leaves spare
    capacity in the sketchpad, which the mind fills with daydreaming that competes with the
    message. Doodling occupies just enough of the sketchpad to block the daydream without
    overloading the loop. Attention, not effort, is the bottleneck — which is the central
    executive's lesson in action.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> doodling is not multitasking that "uses up" attention.
  It looks like one more distraction, so it should hurt recall. It works <em>because</em>
  it occupies an under-used store — the sketchpad — leaving the verbal loop free. Genuinely
  competing verbal tasks (texting while listening) impair recall badly; the benefit is
  specific to tasks that tap a different subsystem.</p>

  <h2>Retrieval: three ways out of the archive</h2>
  <p>
    Storage is only useful if information comes back, and it comes back three ways.
    <strong>Recall</strong> retrieves without cues — an essay exam, remembering a name.
    <strong>Recognition</strong> matches against something in front of you — a
    multiple-choice option, a face in a crowd; it is easier because the cue does half the
    work. <strong>Relearning</strong> shows itself in savings: a language you studied years
    ago and thought gone comes back in a fraction of the original time. The ordering matters
    practically: recognising material, as in re-reading notes, always feels stronger than
    recalling it — and exams mostly demand recall, which is why the feeling is a trap.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> encoding can be automatic or effortful; the desk of working
    memory has specialist parts; and the archive answers to recall, recognition and
    relearning. Next: why the archive leaks, and what demonstrably plugs the leak.
  </p>

  <h2>Forgetting: the curve, and what fights it</h2>
  <p>
    Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) did memory research the hard way: as his own only participant,
    learning lists of meaningless syllables because they came pre-loaded with no meaning to
    confuse things. Re-testing himself across hours and days, he traced the shape that
    almost every memory curve has followed since: loss is steepest in the first hours, then
    flattens into a long tail. His data fit what is now usually drawn as exponential decay —
    retention <strong>R = e<sup>−t/s</sup></strong>, where <em>t</em> is time since study
    and <em>s</em> is a "memory strength" constant — the very curve on the widget above. A
    rough modern echo of his finding: without review, most of what is learned is gone within
    a day, which is why the timing of study matters as much as its quantity.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two strongest fighters are well replicated. <strong>Spacing beats massing</strong>:
    three one-hour sessions spread across a week beat a single three-hour block, because
    each new encounter arrives when the trace has partly decayed, and the extra effort to
    retrieve it strengthens the trace more (the spacing effect, one of psychology's most
    reliable findings). <strong>Testing is studying</strong>: actively retrieving an answer
    — flashcards, closing the book and reconstructing — strengthens memory far more than
    re-reading, which only builds a comfortable feeling of familiarity. And <strong>sleep is
    part of studying</strong>: consolidation, the physical process of stabilising a new
    trace in long-term store, happens largely during sleep, which is why all-nighters
    mostly delete the day's work. Chunking helps a different bottleneck — packing items
    together so seven slots hold more (expert chess players remember whole boards, not 64
    squares; Chase and Simon, 1973). Mnemonic devices — acronyms, acrostics, rhymes —
    work by the same principle: they hand the system a ready-made structure to hang items
    on, and material you connect to yourself (the <strong>self-reference effect</strong>)
    gets the richest web of retrieval cues of all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Forgetting also has a second mechanism besides decay. In <strong>interference</strong>,
    memories compete: <em>proactive</em> interference is old memories blocking new ones
    (last year's password surfacing when you type this year's), and <em>retroactive</em>
    interference is new learning overwriting old (a new phone number crowding out the old
    one). Interference explains a fact decay cannot: why similar material studied close
    together is harder to keep apart than similar material studied days apart.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "re-reading notes is the best way to study." Re-reading
  builds fluency — the words feel familiar, which the brain misreads as knowledge. Testing
  yourself (retrieval practice) beats re-reading in study after study, because the act of
  pulling a memory out strengthens it far more than pushing it in again. Feeling fluent
  and being able to recall are different states, and only one shows up in the exam
  hall.</p>

  <h2>Memory is reconstructive: eyewitnesses and line-ups</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the uncomfortable part: retrieval does not replay a recording; it rebuilds.
    Frederic Bartlett (1932) showed that people rewrite stories to fit their schemas,
    dropping what does not fit and sharpening what does. Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer
    (1974) showed the rewriting happening in real time: participants who watched a car
    crash and were asked how fast the cars were going when they <em>smashed</em> into each
    other estimated higher speeds than those asked about cars that <em>hit</em> — and a
    week later, a third of the "smashed" group remembered broken glass that was never
    there. Every retrieval slightly rewrites the trace, so confident testimony can be
    confidently wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why police line-ups are an experiment about memory, and Joanna Pozzulo's
    <strong>line-up studies</strong> are core syllabus material. Pozzulo and colleagues had
    children (roughly 5–14) and adults watch a video of a staged theft, then try to
    identify the culprit from a line-up. When the real culprit was present, adults were
    somewhat more accurate than children. The dramatic result came from line-ups where the
    culprit was <em>absent</em>: young children chose someone anyway far more often than
    adults did — they picked the look-alike rather than saying "he's not here". Children,
    it seems, treat the task as "pick the person who looks most like the thief", while
    adults are more willing to answer "not present". Pozzulo's practical response —
    teaching children to consider rejection as a real option, and comparing simultaneous
    line-ups with sequential ones — has fed directly into recommendations for real
    interviews with child witnesses. The stakes are enormous: mistaken identification is a
    leading cause of wrongful convictions.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the confident witness is the accurate witness. It feels
  obvious — surely certainty comes from a vivid memory? The research says confidence
  correlates poorly with accuracy and can be inflated by post-event suggestion: a leading
  question, a reassuring line-up officer, even the act of retelling. What predicts accuracy
  is viewing conditions, delay and the absence of leading questions — not certainty.</p>

  <h2>Minds reading minds</h2>
  <p>
    Memory stores the past; cognition also reads other people's present. Understanding that
    others have beliefs, intentions and feelings different from your own — <strong>theory
    of mind</strong> — is a cognitive skill in its own right, and Simon Baron-Cohen and
    colleagues measured it with the <strong>"Reading the Mind in the Eyes" test</strong>
    (2001): 36 photographs of the eye region alone, with four mental-state words (e.g.
    <em>sceptical, playful, irritated, bored</em>) to choose from for each. Adults with
    Asperger syndrome or high-functioning autism scored significantly lower than matched
    controls — typically several points below the control mean of about 26 out of 36 — even
    though their general intelligence was normal, and women scored slightly higher than men
    across groups. The finding localised social-cognitive difficulty to reading others from
    minimal cues, and the test is now used widely (and carefully, since eye-region photos
    differ across cultures) as a measure of cognitive empathy. It sits naturally in this
    lesson because it shows the cognitive method at work: infer an internal process — here,
    mentalising — from performance on a carefully designed task.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the three stores of the multi-store model, and for each give its job, its approximate duration and its capacity.</li>
    <li>You can hum a song while navigating a new city, but you cannot follow a map and read a text message at the same time. Which parts of the working-memory model explain each fact?</li>
    <li>A friend swears re-reading their notes three times is "how I study best". Using the forgetting curve and retrieval practice, what do you tell them?</li>
    <li>An eyewitness is 100% certain the suspect was at the scene. Does that settle it? Give one research-based reason why not.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Sensory store — raw impressions, under a second, very
    large but unprocessed; working memory — conscious workspace, 15–30 seconds without
    rehearsal, about 7 ± 2 items; long-term memory — permanent archive, years, effectively
    unlimited. Information only reaches the archive through attention and elaboration. (2)
    Humming while navigating works because song taps the phonological loop and navigation
    the visuospatial sketchpad — separate stores. Map-reading and text-reading compete
    because both demand the sketchpad (or both demand the central executive's attention) —
    one resource, two claimants. (3) The curve shows most new material decays within a day,
    and re-reading only builds recognition, not the recall exams demand. Spaced sessions
    with self-testing — closing the book and reconstructing — fight decay where it
    happens; the fluency of familiar notes is not knowledge. (4) No. Loftus and Palmer
    showed memory is reconstructed and rewritten by each retrieval and suggestion;
    confidence correlates poorly with accuracy. What matters is viewing conditions, delay
    and leading questions — which is why line-up procedure is designed like an experiment.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The cognitive approach treats the mind as an information-processing system whose internal steps are inferred from behaviour — encoding, storage and retrieval are its three jobs.</li>
    <li>The multi-store model (Atkinson &amp; Shiffrin, 1968): a brief sensory store, a 7 ± 2-item working memory, and a effectively unlimited long-term store.</li>
    <li>Working memory has parts — phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, central executive, episodic buffer — which is why some tasks combine easily and others collide.</li>
    <li>Andrade (2009): doodlers recalled about 29% more, because doodling occupies the sketchpad and blocks daydreaming — evidence for the model's parts.</li>
    <li>Forgetting follows Ebbinghaus's curve; the fighters are spacing, retrieval practice, sleep, chunking and mnemonics. Interference (proactive and retroactive) is the second mechanism.</li>
    <li>Memory is reconstructive: Loftus and Palmer's "smashed" study and Pozzulo's child line-up studies show retrieval rewrites the trace, so confidence is not proof.</li>
    <li>Baron-Cohen's Eyes test extends the cognitive method to social cognition: measure a task, infer the process.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Memory is the first application of the cognitive approach; the unit now widens. The next
    lessons look at perception — how the same information-processing machinery builds the
    visual world — and at cognitive development, Piaget's account of how the machinery
    itself is constructed in childhood. Later lessons return to the classic studies introduced
    here and put them under the methodological microscope: sampling, controls and ethics.
    The through-line never changes — design a task, measure the behaviour, infer the process.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A few layers below the model: <strong>levels of processing</strong> (Craik and Lockhart,
    1972) argues that stores matter less than depth of encoding — judging whether
    <em>cra</em> rhymes with <em>cot</em> produces far worse recall than judging whether it
    fits "We saw a crazy cat", even though rehearsal time is identical. Meaning, not
    repetition, builds <em>s</em>. <strong>Encoding specificity</strong> (Tulving) adds that
    retrieval succeeds when cues match the encoding context, which is why recalling a fact
    in the exam hall can feel harder than recalling it in the library where you read it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Long-term memory, it turns out, is not a single system. <strong>Explicit</strong>
    (declarative) memory — what you can declare — splits into <em>episodic</em> memory for
    events and <em>semantic</em> memory for facts; <strong>implicit</strong> (non-declarative)
    memory includes <em>procedural</em> memory for skills and learned associations, and never
    enters consciousness. The classic case of H.M., whose hippocampus was removed in 1953 to
    treat epilepsy, revealed the wiring: he lost the ability to form new conscious memories,
    yet could still learn mirror-drawing skills day after day with no memory of practising —
    implicit learning ran on while explicit filing stopped. Consolidation runs on repeating
    ripples of hippocampal activity during sleep, the mechanism behind "sleep is part of
    studying". At the cellular level, long-term potentiation (LTP) — a synapse that is
    repeatedly co-activated becomes permanently easier to trigger — is the leading candidate
    for what a memory physically is. And at the frontier, researchers ask whether each
    reconsolidation window (the minutes after retrieval when a trace is writable again)
    could be used to weaken traumatic memories. The 1968 model is still the map; the
    expedition is far from over.
  </p>

